# Imprint — Persistent Memory (use automatically, every session)

> Copy this file to `.cursor/rules/imprint.mdc` in your project (or add it as a
> User Rule in Cursor Settings). `alwaysApply: true` keeps it active every turn.

You have **Imprint** memory tools via the `imprint` MCP server:
`get_memories`, `save_memory`, `search_memories`, `pin_memory`, `summarize_session`.

Cursor has no automatic "save after every response" hook (that exists only in Claude
Code). So **you** must capture memory proactively — do not wait for the user to say
"save" or "remember this". Follow these rules silently.

## 1. Session start
Call `get_memories` with the user's first message as the query **before** answering.
Use the results to personalize. Don't announce it.

## 2. Save proactively (do NOT wait to be told)
Call `save_memory` **immediately** when you learn a durable fact — projects, goals,
deadlines, decisions, progress (done/pending/blocked/next step), preferences (style,
tools, libraries), or personal/work facts (name, role, stack, background).
Pick a `topic`: `work | projects | preferences | personal | health | general`.
One clear fact per memory. Never say "I'll remember that" — just save and continue.

## 3. Session-end triggers
On **bye / done / stop / finish / that's all / wrap up**, save: what was completed
(`topic: projects`), the current state (pin it), the next step (pin it), any blocker.

## 4. Personal questions require a lookup first
Before answering anything about the user (health, preferences, past, background,
goals), call `search_memories` with their question first. Never answer from
assumptions.

## 5. Checkpoint on milestones
After a bug fixed, feature shipped, decision, commit push, or deploy — save a
one-line checkpoint of the current state.

Do not save throwaway details, anything the user says to forget, or secrets. If a
new fact contradicts an old memory, save the new one.
